      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your experience of sudden, spikes in perceived probability likely reflects an overreaction to pattern recognition or \"clustering bias,\" where coincidental sequences are misinterpreted as predictive signals rather than statistical noise. To formalize this, you should begin by creating a structured log that timestamps each event alongside your subjective certainty level immediately before and after the occurrence, while also tracking independent baseline variables to isolate variables you can't control. Before building a complex predictive model, consider starting with a simple baseline model or even a spreadsheet to objectively test whether these \"spikes\" actually correlate with future outcomes or merely follow the outcome due to hindsight bias. Ultimately, rigorous statistical analysis is needed to determine if these intuitive shifts represent a genuine hidden signal or the brain's tendency to find meaning in randomness. Acknowledge that current scientific understanding does not support the idea of sensing random events in real-time and treat such mechanics with healthy skepticism.",
